    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Field observations indicate that heterogeneous material properties associated with sedimentary layering can affect the geometry and slip distributions of normal faults. Layers that are referred to as \"less competent,\" \"less brittle,\" or \"less stiff\" are often associated with reductions in fault slip and/or fault terminations. We show that non-uniform stresses associated with the uniform tectonic extension of layered rocks may result in more compressive horizontal stresses in soft layers, thereby increasing the frictional resistance to faulting. Consequently, layers with lesser elastic moduli may be less likely to slip. Numerical models are used to address how slip accumulates on frictional faults that have propagated through layers with different material properties subjected to a uniform extension. Our analysis is supported by field measurements that indicate stresses are dependent on lithology.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
